<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-Taylor-series" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Taylor Series</title>

  <p>We have seen that power series can be useful, and that we can determine where
  they converge.  However, a more difficult question in general is determining
  what they converge <em>to</em>.
  </p>

  <example>
    <p>Consider the power series <m>\displaystyle f(x)=\sum _{n=0} ^\infty x^n</m>.  Note that for <m>|r|\lt 1</m>,
    then <m>f(r)</m> is a geometric series, so <m>f(r)=\frac{1}{1-r}</m>.  In other words,
    on the interval of convergence <m>(-1,1)</m> we have <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=0} ^\infty x^n = \frac{1}{1-x}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Note that we can easily compute  the derivatives of <m>f(x)</m> at <m>0</m> using <xref ref="thm-power-series-diff-integral"/>:
      <m>f^{(n)}(0)=n!</m>.  As a consequence, the Taylor polynomials are
      <m>\displaystyle T_k(x) = \sum _{n=0} ^k \frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!}x^n = \sum _{n=0} ^k x^n</m>.
    </p>
  </example>

  <definition xml:id="def-taylor-series">
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>f(x)</m> is infinitely differentiable, then the <term>Taylor series</term> for <m>f(x)</m> centered at <m>x=c</m> is
      <md>\sum _{n=0} ^\infty \dfrac{f^{(n)}(c)}{n!}(x-c)^n</md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a function is equal to its Taylor series near a point, it is called <term>analytic</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>Not every infintely differentiable function is analytic, but it turns out the common
    ones we work with are.</p>
  </remark>

  <activity>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine the Taylor series for <m>e^x</m> centered at <m>x=0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine the Taylor series for <m>\sin(x)</m> centered at <m>x=0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine the Taylor series for <m>\cos(x)</m> centered at <m>x=0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </activity>

  <example>
    <p>Knowing these Taylor series is very useful.  For example, we may easily compute
      <md>
        <mrow> \lim _{x \rightarrow 0} \dfrac{\sin(x)}{x}\amp =\lim _{x \rightarrow} \frac{1}{x}\sum _{n=0} ^\infty \dfrac{(-1)^{n}x^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp =\lim _{x \rightarrow} \sum _{n=0} ^\infty \dfrac{(-1)^{n}x^{2n}}{(2n+1)!} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = 1</mrow>
      </md>
      This is much easier than the traditional geometric argument.
    </p>
  </example>

  <example>
    <p>
      We can generate new Taylor series from ones we already know this way.  For example, since we know
      <m>\displaystyle e^{x}=\sum _{n=0} ^\infty \dfrac{x^n}{n!}</m>, then
      <md> e^{x^2} = \sum _{n=0} ^\infty \dfrac{(x^2)^n}{n!} = \sum _{n=0} ^\infty x^{2n}{n!}</md>.
    </p>
  </example>

  <activity>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use Taylor series to compute <m>\displaystyle \int \sin(x^2)\ dx</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </activity>




</section>